    "q": "What is a North Star metric and how would you define one for a food delivery app?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "North Star Metric: \"High-quality orders completed per week\" - orders delivered on-time with ≥4-star ratings from returning users.\n\nWhy this works:\nA North Star must capture value delivered, not value extracted. Revenue and GMV are lagging indicators. This metric forces simultaneous optimization of frequency (habit formation), reliability (on-time delivery), and satisfaction - when all three grow together, retention and margins follow structurally.\n\nData validation first:\nBefore committing, I'd run 90-day cohort analysis confirming that users receiving on-time, highly-rated orders show meaningfully higher 30-day retention than those who don't. If correlation is weak (r < 0.4), the metric needs revision - possibly splitting into separate supply-side and demand-side leading indicators.\n\nPilot design:\nFour-week geo-split test: one market optimizes dispatch algorithms and restaurant partnerships explicitly around this combined metric; control market optimizes for raw order volume. Primary comparison: 30-day retention and contribution margin per user.\n\nSuccess metrics:\n- North Star grows 8–12% MoM\n- 30-day retention improves ≥5 percentage points\n- Contribution margin per active user increases quarter-over-quarter\n- Churn rate decreases without equivalent CAC increase\n\nCore trade-off:\nOptimizing for quality orders will suppress total volume short-term - you'll exit low-quality restaurant partnerships and tighten delivery windows, hurting GMV optics. That's the correct call for a post-PMF business prioritizing sustainable unit economics. The risk is internal pressure from stakeholders anchored to volume metrics, which requires explicit executive alignment before adopting this framework.",
