    "q": "How would you handle a situation where user feedback conflicts with your product vision?",
    "a": "Framework: Treat the conflict as a hypothesis test, not a debate to win.\n\nFirst, I validate the signal. Is this feedback from 3 vocal users or a pattern across support tickets, NPS responses, and churn interviews? One enterprise customer requesting a dashboard tweak is noise. Forty users abandoning the core workflow is a falsified assumption worth investigating.\n\nSecond, I separate their problem from their proposed solution. Users accurately describe pain; they rarely diagnose root cause. If users say \"we need bulk export,\" the real issue might be workflow friction I can solve more elegantly within the vision.\n\nThird, I make the conflict explicit with my team: \"Users want X, vision says Y, here's my read.\" Hidden conflicts produce bad decisions.\n\nIf tension persists, I run a targeted test. If users resist our opinionated onboarding flow, I'd A/B test \"guided\" vs. \"flexible\" variants across 1,000 new signups over 30 days. Success metrics: Day-7 retention, time-to-first-value, and support ticket volume per cohort. If the flexible variant wins meaningfully on retention without increasing support load, the vision assumption is wrong and we update it. No monument-building.\n\nTrade-offs I'm explicitly managing:\n- Over-indexing on feedback → incoherent, committee-built product that satisfies no one deeply\n- Over-indexing on vision → building confidently in the wrong direction, burning runway on elegant solutions to problems users don't have\n\nVision is a hypothesis. Feedback is evidence that sharpens or breaks it. My job is running that process rigorously, not protecting either side.",
