    "q": "Describe how you would run a competitive analysis for a new product.",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Framework: Competitive analysis is a positioning tool, not a feature matrix.\n\nFive steps, assuming customer access and a two-week timeline.\n\nStep 1: Define the competitive set correctly.\nDirect competitors are obvious. Real threats are indirect competitors (same job-to-be-done, different solution) and substitutes-spreadsheets, manual workflows, doing nothing.\n\nStep 2: Research from the user's perspective.\nUse each product as a real user. Mine 3-4 star reviews on G2 and App Store-not 1s or 5s, which are emotional outliers. Interview competitors' customers via LinkedIn or user communities. Track changelogs to reverse-engineer investment priorities.\n\nStep 3: Map dimensions your target segment actually weights.\nCore job completion, onboarding friction, cost, and speed-scored against what customers say matters, not what you assume.\n\nStep 4: Identify white space.\nWhere do all competitors consistently underperform on high-importance dimensions? That's your entry point, not where you're marginally better.\n\nStep 5: Validate before committing.\nSynthesize into a positioning hypothesis: *\"We win because [capability] for [segment] struggling with [problem].\"* Run an A/B test-two messaging variants across ten sales conversations (five each), measuring conversion rate and objection frequency as primary metrics, time-to-close as secondary.\n\nData validation: Require at least three independent sources (reviews, interviews, usage data) before treating any gap as real.\n\nTrade-off: Broad competitive coverage versus deep ownership of one dimension. For a new product, narrow wins-you cannot out-resource incumbents across the board.\n\nOutput: One-pager. Competitive map, validated gaps, positioning hypothesis. Not a 50-slide deck nobody reads.",
