    "q": "How would you roll out a major feature to 10 million users?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Gate-based phased rollout with hard exit criteria defined before launch-not during the incident.\n\nFirst, validate context: Is monitoring instrumented? What's the blast radius? Is this net-new or a redesign? I'll assume new feature, observability in place, contained failure scope.\n\nNever ship to 10M simultaneously. You lose containment and attribution.\n\nPhase 1 – Internal (Week 1): Employees only. Surfaces crashes and UX confusion before real users are exposed.\n\nPhase 2 – Controlled A/B test (Weeks 2–3): 1–5% treatment vs. control. Pre-define hard kill switches: pause if crash rate exceeds 1%, support volume spikes >30% above baseline, or activation falls below hypothesis threshold. These numbers get locked before launch-not negotiated mid-panic.\n\nPhase 3 – Staged expansion (Weeks 4–7): 5% → 10% → 25% → 50%. Hold 24–48 hours at each gate. Feature flags must support sub-30-minute rollback-verify this before Phase 2 begins.\n\nPhase 4 – Full release: 50% stability unlocks 100%, paired with targeted in-app prompts to drive adoption.\n\nSuccess metrics: crash rate (<0.5%), feature activation rate, p95 error rate in the first 4 hours post-expansion, support ticket delta, and the core engagement metric tied to the feature's intended outcome.\n\nKey trade-off: Speed versus safety. Phased rollout adds 4–6 weeks but prevents one bad deploy from reaching 10M users. Rollback itself carries risk-users who adopted the feature experience regression. Mitigate with transparent in-app messaging.\n\nAssign the pause decision to one named person before launch. Ambiguity here is how incidents escalate.",
