    "q": "How would you improve the onboarding experience for a B2B SaaS productivity tool that's losing 60% of new users in the first week?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Diagnose Before Building\n\n60% week-one churn signals activation failure-users aren't reaching the core value moment before quitting. I'd prioritize diagnosis over shipping solutions.\n\nData Validation First (72 hours, no code)\n\nPull funnel drop-off by step, watch 20 session recordings segmented by role, and deploy a single exit survey: *\"What stopped you from continuing?\"* Categorize responses into friction, confusion, or missing value. Without this, any solution risks optimizing the wrong step.\n\nRoot Cause Hypotheses\n- Too many setup steps before experiencing core value\n- One-size-fits-all flow ignoring admin vs. contributor needs\n- No clear \"next action\" signal after initial setup\n\nInterventions (prioritized by impact-to-effort)\n1. Role-fork at signup: One question routes users to tailored flows immediately\n2. Engineer the \"first win\": Identify the action predicting D7 retention (e.g., \"task assigned\")-redesign session one entirely around reaching it within 5 minutes\n3. Progressive disclosure: Surface 3 features in week one; unlock additional functionality as users hit milestones\n\nPilot Design\n\nA/B test role-based onboarding against control with 20% of new signups over 3 weeks-enough volume for statistical significance without overexposing users to an unvalidated experience.\n\nSuccess Metrics\n- Primary: D7 retention (target: 40% → 55%+)\n- Secondary: Time-to-first-key-action, support ticket volume, week-two feature adoption\n\nTrade-off Awareness\n\nHigh-touch outreach for inactive enterprise trials improves activation but doesn't scale. Prioritize self-serve improvements first; reserve human outreach for accounts exceeding a defined revenue threshold.",
