    "q": "How would you design a notification system for a mobile app that doesn't feel spammy?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Earn the right to interrupt, or don't interrupt.\n\nEvery notification must pass the \"worth waking me up for\" test. If it can't clear that bar, it doesn't get sent.\n\nBefore designing, I'd validate:\n- Opt-out rate by notification type (>30% all-disabled signals broken trust)\n- Open rates segmented by time-of-day and user cohort\n- Which notification types correlate with re-engagement vs. churn\n\nSystem Design (social product):\n\nThree-tier taxonomy with differentiated defaults:\n- Transactional (on by default): user-initiated triggers-\"upload complete,\" \"payment confirmed\"\n- Social (configurable): others' actions-batched into one daily digest, not individual pushes\n- Promotional (opt-in only): always in-app banners, never push\n\nBehavioral suppression: If a user ignores the same notification type 5 consecutive times, auto-suppress and surface a preference prompt inline-don't make them hunt through settings.\n\nHard cap: 3 push notifications/day, configurable upward by user preference.\n\nPilot: A/B test batched social digests vs. real-time individual pushes across 10% of users for 30 days.\n\nSuccess metrics: 30-day notification opt-in retention, CTR by notification type, opt-out rate, and 7-day re-engagement rate post-notification.\n\nCore tradeoffs:\n- Batching reduces noise but weakens real-time social gratification loops\n- Conservative defaults protect long-term trust but suppress short-term DAU lift\n- Behavioral suppression improves experience but reduces legitimate re-engagement reach\n\nThe right call: optimize for 90-day retention over 7-day DAU-trust compounds, spam doesn't.",
