    "q": "What would a good MVP look like for a peer-to-peer tutoring marketplace?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Framework: Validate liquidity before building features.\n\nThe core bet is threefold: students reliably find qualified tutors, tutors earn meaningfully, and the platform creates enough coordination value that both sides prefer it over WhatsApp groups or Craigslist.\n\nData validation first. Post 20 fake tutor listings on a university subreddit and measure booking intent via Typeform. Threshold: if fewer than 15% of visitors submit interest, urgency is insufficient to justify building.\n\nMVP scope (one campus, math/CS only, student tutors):\n\n*Students:* subject + availability search, tutor profiles with ratings, in-platform booking and Stripe payments.\n\n*Tutors:* profile creation, availability calendar, session notifications, Stripe payouts.\n\n*Explicitly excluded:* native video (Zoom links suffice), AI matching, mobile app, subscriptions.\n\nPilot: Launch at one university, targeting 100 tutors and 2,000 students within 60 days. A/B test tutor onboarding-cohort A gets self-serve signup, cohort B gets a 10-minute assisted setup call. Primary measured outcome: profile completion rate and first-session conversion. This isolates whether supply acquisition is a friction problem or a motivation problem.\n\nSuccess metrics (60-day window): 50 completed sessions, average rating ≥4.2/5, tutor retention ≥60% after first session, student rebooking rate ≥30%.\n\nTrade-offs:\n- Geographic focus builds liquidity; breadth kills it\n- Tutor supply is the binding constraint-solve supply before marketing to students\n- Stripe adds onboarding friction but is non-negotiable for trust\n\nHit metrics → expand to campus two. Miss metrics → the model is broken; pivot before scaling.",
