    "q": "How would you redesign the Instagram Explore page to better serve users?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Recommendation: Redesign Explore around explicit intent-splitting-separating passive discovery from active search-rather than optimizing for scroll depth.\n\nCore problem: Explore conflates two distinct user jobs: \"surprise me\" (passive discovery) and \"find X\" (active search). The current 3-column grid maximizes engagement time, not user value, creating a disconnect between Instagram's growth goals and actual user satisfaction.\n\nData validation first:\n- What % of Explore sessions include a search query vs. pure browsing?\n- What's the follow/save rate relative to scroll depth? (Hypothesis: low action rate despite high time-spent)\n- Where do users abandon-entry, mid-session, or post-engagement?\n\nProposed redesign:\n1. Dual-mode entry: Explicit \"For You\" and \"Search\" modes eliminate intent ambiguity\n2. 2-column grid with topic labels: Forces algorithmic quality over quantity\n3. Algorithmic transparency: \"Recommended because you saved @ChefName's post\" improves trust and relevance signals\n4. Triggered CTAs: After 5+ seconds on a post, surface \"Follow creator\" or \"See similar\" to convert passive interest into action\n\nA/B test: 5% rollout, holding ad impressions constant. Primary metric: follows and saves per session. Secondary metric: 7-day retention of new follows (quality signal). Guardrail: time-spent decline capped at 10%.\n\nKey trade-off: Deeper personalization creates filter bubbles, narrowing discovery. Mitigate with a \"Trending outside your network\" module preserving serendipity without sacrificing relevance.",
