    "q": "How would you design a habit-tracking feature for a fitness app?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Design for cue → routine → reward, validated before building.\n\nFirst, validate the core assumption: are beginners dropping off due to logging friction or motivation failure? I'd run 12 user interviews with D7–D30 churners and pull retention cohort data to confirm whether D30 habit-formers retain at 2x+ through D90. If that delta doesn't exist, the feature doesn't justify roadmap priority.\n\nCore design decisions:\n\n1. Cue: User sets an explicit trigger (\"after work, Monday/Wednesday/Friday\"). Vague intentions fail; implementation intentions have strong behavioral evidence behind them.\n2. Routine: One-tap home screen widget check-in, under 3 seconds. Requiring app launch kills compliance for beginners specifically.\n3. Reward: Streak counter (loss aversion), weekly reframe (\"2 of 3-close it next week?\"), and opt-in friend accountability pairing.\n4. Failure recovery: Replace \"streak broken\" with \"Personal best: 23 days.\" Shame kills re-engagement.\n\nPilot: A/B test streak-only vs. streak + social accountability with 1,000 users per arm over 6 weeks.\n\nSuccess metrics:\n- Primary: D30 habit completion rate (≥3 of 4 weeks hitting goal)\n- Secondary: D90 retention delta between habit-formers vs. non-formers\n- Guardrail: uninstall rate (social pressure can backfire)\n\nTrade-offs: One-tap logging sacrifices data richness for compliance-correct call for beginners. Social accountability lifts motivation but risks anxiety-driven churn, which is why it's opt-in only and monitored via the guardrail metric.",
