    "q": "How would you improve the LinkedIn job search experience?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Recommendation: Improve match quality over search volume-LinkedIn's core problem is low signal-to-noise, not missing features.\n\nData Validation First\nBefore building anything, I'd instrument current behavior: What's the application-to-interview rate? Where does drop-off occur between search results and submissions? My hypothesis is users see irrelevant results, over-apply defensively, and get poor outcomes-creating a trust death spiral. I'd validate through session recordings and cohort analysis comparing power users against churned users within 90 days of signup.\n\nThree Prioritized Problems\n1. Relevance gap – Keyword matching ignores career trajectory; junior candidates surface senior roles because \"manager\" appeared historically\n2. Fit opacity – No qualification signal before applying drives spray-and-pray behavior\n3. ATS abandonment – External redirects break momentum; approximately 60% of mobile users drop off mid-application\n\nProposed A/B Test\nShip \"Fit Score\" to 10% of active job seekers: display \"You match 8/10 required skills; gaps: Python, SQL\" on each listing. Run for 6 weeks against control.\n\nSuccess Metrics\n- Apply-to-interview rate (primary north star)\n- Applications per session (expect decrease-intentional)\n- 30-day return search rate (trust proxy)\n- Recruiter-reported candidate quality score (lagging validation)\n\nTrade-offs\nOver-personalization kills serendipity and suppresses career pivots. I'd cap match-based ranking at 70% of results, preserving 30% for adjacent roles outside current trajectory.\n\nGoal: fewer, better applications-quality conversion outperforms volume across every layer of LinkedIn's recruiter revenue model.",
