    "q": "How would you design a rating and review system for a two-sided marketplace?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Recommendation: Build a bidirectional, simultaneous-reveal, structured rating system-validate assumptions before full rollout.\n\nData validation first: Pull three signals before designing anything-funnel dropout rate at trust decision points, post-transaction support ticket themes, and whether low review completion correlates with repeat purchase drop-off. These determine whether the core problem is trust acquisition or trust accuracy.\n\nCore design:\n\n1. Simultaneous reveal - Both parties submit before either sees the other's rating. Eliminates retaliation bias that corrupts asymmetric systems.\n2. Structured dimensions - \"Arrived on time: Yes/No,\" \"Matched description: Yes/No,\" \"Would transact again: Yes/No.\" Combats grade inflation where 95% of ratings cluster at 4.8–5.0, rendering stars useless.\n3. Verified transactions only - Review requires a completed transaction ID. Eliminates incentivized and retaliatory spam at the source.\n4. Interpretable display - \"94% on-time across 200 transactions\" outperforms \"4.9 stars\" for decision accuracy and scans faster.\n5. Seller response window - Public replies to negative reviews; credible responses often recover more trust than the review damaged.\n\nPilot: A/B test structured Yes/No dimensions versus traditional 5-star across two matched seller cohorts for 60 days.\n\nSuccess metrics: Review completion rate, rating-to-repeat-purchase correlation, and trust-driven conversion lift per cohort.\n\nKey trade-offs: Structured ratings reduce manipulation but sacrifice nuance. Simultaneous reveal improves honesty but reduces completion rates. I'd launch verified and structured, measure completion drop-off, then add optional free-text only where completion holds.",
