    "q": "How would you measure whether a search feature is performing well?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Measuring Search Performance\n\nFramework: Search succeeds when users find what they came for - not when they search more.\n\nStart with data validation. Before trusting any metric, audit the top 20 queries manually. Do results make sense? Algorithms mask obvious failures. Confirm tracking captures the full funnel: query → click → post-click behavior. Missing instrumentation invalidates everything downstream.\n\nFive metrics that matter:\n\n1. Search abandonment rate - users who search then exit without clicking. Best single signal; segment by query type.\n2. Click position distribution - consistent clicks below position 3 mean top results are wrong.\n3. Query reformulation rate - immediate re-search signals the first attempt failed.\n4. Post-click dwell time - short dwell after clicking indicates irrelevant results.\n5. Zero-results rate - flags content gaps or broken query parsing.\n\nPilot: Establish baseline abandonment rate on your top 10 queries by volume. Run an A/B test improving relevance ranking exclusively on those queries. Measure abandonment rate, reformulation rate, and downstream conversion over two weeks. Statistical significance is achievable faster on high-traffic search than most product surfaces.\n\nCore trade-off: Recall vs. precision. Returning everything inflates clicks but destroys trust over time. For consumer products, prioritize precision - three highly relevant results outperform 100 mediocre ones. Accept lower raw click volume if post-click conversion and dwell time improve together.\n\nWhat to ignore: Total search volume and raw CTR. Both reward poor relevance and create false confidence in a broken experience.",
