    "q": "How would you approach A/B testing a major redesign of a product's homepage?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Staged component testing with holdback validation\n\nBefore writing a single test spec, I'd validate the hypothesis with data: pull current homepage analytics to identify where users drop off-scroll depth, click heatmaps, exit rates by segment. This tells us *which* components are underperforming and prevents testing a redesign that doesn't address the actual problem.\n\nTesting approach:\n\nRather than shipping the full redesign as one A/B test-which conflates signals and creates attribution chaos-I'd run staged component tests: headline, hero, CTA, and navigation independently. Each test owns one primary metric: sign-up rate. Secondary diagnostic metrics include scroll depth, time-on-page, and CTA click-through rate.\n\nPilot structure:\n\n- Days 1–2: 5% traffic split to catch rendering bugs and technical breaks\n- Days 3–14: Expand to 50/50 with a 10% holdback on the control continuing through week 3\n\nThe holdback is critical. Major redesigns trigger novelty confusion in days 1–7-users behave erratically simply because something looks different. A standard 2-week test catches the dip and misses the recovery. The holdback captures the stable long-term signal we actually want to act on.\n\nGuardrails: Pre-define automatic rollback triggers before launch-crash rate increases >0.5%, or sign-ups drop >15% in any key demographic segment. Non-negotiable, not a judgment call mid-test.\n\nTrade-offs I'm accepting: Component testing requires 6–8 weeks versus shipping immediately. I accept that delay because it gives us conviction on *what* drove lift, not just *that* lift occurred-essential for a redesign that becomes the foundation we build on for years.",
