    "q": "How would you design a waitlist and early access strategy for a new consumer app?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Waitlist = Demand Signal + Quality Gate + Viral Loop\n\nBefore designing anything, I'd validate three assumptions: Are we building hype or controlling rollout quality? What's our timeline? Do we have an existing audience? I'll assume both goals, 90-day horizon, zero existing audience.\n\nDesign Blueprint:\n\n1. Sharp value prop on signup page. One sentence: \"[Product] helps you [specific job].\" Ambiguity kills conversion-test two variants on day one.\n\n2. Referral-powered queue. \"You're #4,372. Refer 3 friends to jump 500 spots.\" Robinhood's referral mechanics became their primary pre-launch acquisition engine-replicable with modest engineering investment.\n\n3. Graduated access waves. Week 1: top 100 referrers. Week 2: next 500. Week 3: next 2,000. Sustained urgency outperforms a single drop event.\n\n4. Pre-launch nurture sequence. Weekly emails, sneak-peek videos, one-question surveys. Invested users activate and retain measurably better at launch.\n\n5. Hard 90-day cap. Intent evaporates beyond 90 days. If you're not ready, delay opening the waitlist-don't burn the demand signal early.\n\nValidation Step: Before full commitment, survey 200 signups within 48 hours-measure willingness-to-pay and primary use case to confirm product-market assumptions hold.\n\nA/B Test: Two-cell test-referral incentive vs. no incentive-measuring signup-to-referral conversion within 14 days before scaling.\n\nSuccess Metrics: Waitlist-to-activated-user conversion (target >40%), D30 retention for waitlist cohort vs. organic (hypothesis: 15-20% higher due to stronger intent), and referral K-factor above 0.5.\n\nCore Trade-off: Referral mechanics optimize for volume, not quality-early users may be demographically skewed and unrepresentative. Acceptable for bug-catching, but weight qualitative feedback from intentional applicants more heavily during wave analysis.",
    "difficulty": "Easy",
    "domain": "general",
