    "q": "How would you design a help and support experience within a mobile app?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Tiered support with contextual delivery, optimized for self-service first.\n\nBefore designing anything, I'd validate the actual problem: analyze support ticket categories, session recordings around help-seeking behavior, and CSAT scores to pinpoint where users genuinely fail. Assuming the dominant pattern is feature confusion in a SaaS app with a lean support team.\n\nFour-tier channel hierarchy:\n\n1. Prevention - Inline tooltips and smart empty states eliminate questions before they form. Context-triggered, never modal-heavy.\n2. Self-service - Searchable help center surfacing top 10 issues (60-70% of tickets are typically repeats). Contextual delivery: tapping Help during checkout surfaces checkout-specific articles only.\n3. In-app async chat - Native, never redirecting to browser. Reserved for questions requiring back-and-forth.\n4. Email - Complex or sensitive issues exclusively.\n\nPilot: A/B test contextual help versus generic help center across the three highest-ticket flows. Four-week run, 1,000 sessions per variant, segmented by new versus returning users.\n\nSuccess metrics: First-contact resolution rate (primary), time-to-resolution, human escalation rate, and help article deflection rate.\n\nTrade-offs I'd navigate consciously:\n- Self-service scales well but breaks on nuanced issues - human support should be a deliberate layer, not a fallback\n- Comprehensive FAQs reduce knowledge gaps but increase navigation friction; prioritize depth on high-volume issues over broad coverage\n\nFeedback loop: Monthly ticket analysis feeds directly into the product backlog. Recurring support themes signal UX failures, not support failures - and should be treated accordingly.",
