    "q": "What would you prioritize if you had one quarter to meaningfully improve retention for a casual mobile game?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Diagnose first, then execute one high-confidence lever at scale.\n\nWeek 1 - Data validation before building anything:\nPull retention curves by cohort to pinpoint the exact churn cliff (D1, D7, D30). Compare behavioral fingerprints of retained vs. churned users - session frequency, levels completed, and time-between-sessions. Run a single-question exit survey: \"What made you stop playing?\" One week of diagnosis prevents shipping the wrong fix entirely.\n\nAssuming D7 cliff (most common in casual games):\n\nPriority 1 - Difficulty calibration (Weeks 2–5):\nAnalyze level-completion funnels. If any level shows 35%+ drop-off before the habit window closes, that's the highest-leverage fix. Smooth the curve or insert a scaffolded step. Players who can't progress don't return - no reward loop fixes a broken core loop.\n\nPriority 2 - Daily login rewards (Weeks 3–6):\nMeaningful currency or consumables, not cosmetic fluff. Creates an explicit re-engagement trigger for players who *have* cleared the difficulty barrier. Target: +5–8 points on D7 retention.\n\nPriority 3 - Contextual push notifications (Week 5):\n\"You're one move away from beating level 8.\" A/B test timing: morning vs. evening sends on lapsed users (3–7 day gap). Expected recovery: 5–8% at near-zero marginal cost.\n\nPilot structure:\nLaunch difficulty smoothing to 25% of new installs. Gate full rollout on D14 retention improving +5 points within 3 weeks.\n\nPrimary metric: D14 retention. Secondary: Level-completion rate through session 5, weekly sessions per active user.\n\nKey trade-off: Difficulty reduction risks boring skilled players - segment casual vs. power users before shipping globally.",
