    "q": "How would you design a product for elderly users who are new to smartphones?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Recommendation: Design a single-purpose communication device with progressive complexity unlocked by family-assisted setup.\n\nValidate before building. Run 10 contextual interviews with users 70+ across tech-phobic and tech-curious segments, observing physical interaction patterns-grip, tap accuracy, reading distance. This prevents designing for hypothetical users.\n\nCore principles for communication-first design:\n\n- One screen, one action. Home screen shows one large button: \"Call [Name].\" No menus, no navigation. Cognitive load is the primary failure mode.\n- 72pt minimum touch targets. Standard 44pt fails with hand tremors. Buttons must visibly depress with haptic confirmation-silent taps create repeat-tapping spirals.\n- Words over icons. \"Search\" beats a magnifying glass. Eliminate metaphors unfamiliar to non-digital natives.\n- Always recoverable. Confirm before irreversible actions. Persistent \"Go Home\" button. Undo for accidental swipes.\n- Family setup mode. Remote configuration with usage visibility (\"Mom made 3 calls this week\") reduces abandonment caused by unsupported confusion.\n\nPilot: A/B test family-assisted setup vs. guided self-setup across 200 users in assisted living facilities over 4 weeks.\n\nSuccess metrics: Task completion rate (>85%), accidental tap rate (<10%), 30-day retention (>60%), family NPS (>40).\n\nTrade-offs: Radical simplicity limits feature expansion and monetization. Elderly-specific design also diverges from broader disability accessibility standards-conflating these creates dangerous design assumptions requiring separate tracks.",
