    "id": "apm-prod-41",
    "q": "How would you decide whether to build a feature using AI or traditional deterministic logic?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Evaluate across three dimensions-consequence severity, pattern complexity, and data availability-before writing a single line of code.\n\nData validation first: Pull the last 90 days of support tickets and edge-case logs. If >20% of cases fall outside existing rule coverage, AI becomes viable. If labeled training data is under 1,000 examples, default to deterministic logic regardless of complexity signals.\n\nUse deterministic logic when:\n- Output is legally or financially binding (pricing, permissions, compliance)\n- Exhaustive rules can be written and maintained in under a day\n- Auditability is non-negotiable for regulatory or audit purposes\n\nUse AI when:\n- Language, intent, or fuzzy matching is core to the feature\n- Edge cases multiply faster than engineering can codify\n- Maintenance burden of brittle rules exceeds model retraining cost\n\nPilot design: Run shadow-mode testing for two weeks-deterministic logic handles live decisions while the AI model runs in parallel, logging outputs without serving them. This eliminates user risk while generating direct comparison data.\n\nSuccess metrics: Edge-case resolution rate, false positive rate, p95 latency delta, and engineer hours-to-update when requirements change.\n\nTrade-off awareness: AI buys adaptability and reduces long-term maintenance cost but sacrifices explainability and introduces non-deterministic failure modes that are harder to debug under pressure. For high-stakes paths, the right architecture is often deterministic logic as the decision core with AI as a non-blocking signal layer-not a binary choice between the two.",
    "difficulty": "Easy",
    "domain": "general",
    "tracks": [
      "Technical PM",
      "Data / AI PM"
    ]
