    "id": "apm-prod-42",
    "q": "What is product-led growth (PLG) and how would you explain it to a non-technical stakeholder?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Framework: PLG is a go-to-market strategy where the product itself drives acquisition, retention, and expansion-reducing reliance on traditional sales motions.\n\nFor a non-technical stakeholder: \"Think of Slack or Dropbox. You sign up free, get value immediately, invite teammates, and eventually your company pays. No sales call required. The product does the selling.\"\n\nWhy it matters commercially: PLG compresses CAC because users self-onboard and virally recruit others. But I'd validate before recommending it.\n\nData validation first: Measure current time-to-value (TTV). Can users reach their \"aha moment\" independently in under 5 minutes? Pull drop-off rates at each onboarding step. If >60% abandon before activation, PLG will fail without product investment first.\n\nPilot: Run a 60-day A/B test-Cohort A gets sales-assisted onboarding, Cohort B gets self-serve with in-product guidance (tooltips, checklists). Compare activation rate, 30-day retention, and expansion revenue per cohort.\n\nSuccess metrics: TTV, activation rate, viral coefficient (invites per activated user), and PQL-to-paid conversion rate.\n\nTrade-offs I'd flag explicitly:\n- PLG favors high-volume, lower-ARPU segments; enterprise deals typically still need a sales overlay\n- Requires significant upfront product investment to eliminate friction\n- Works best for horizontal tools; complex vertical products often need hybrid PLG + sales-assist\n\nRecommendation: Pursue PLG if TTV is under 5 minutes and viral coefficient exceeds 0.3. Otherwise, fix activation first-launching PLG on a leaky funnel accelerates churn, not growth.",
