    "id": "apm-prod-47",
    "q": "How would you handle a situation where the sprint backlog keeps changing mid-sprint due to executive requests?",
    "a": "Recommendation: Mid-sprint churn is an upstream alignment failure. Fix the intake process, not the sprint mechanics.\n\nValidate before acting. I'd instrument two sprints immediately: log every mid-sprint request by source, urgency classification (genuine emergency vs. reprioritization), and business justification. My hypothesis: 75-80% are reprioritization, not emergencies. Data either confirms the structural fix or reveals a different root cause entirely.\n\nStructural intervention: Implement a formal intake gate with three non-negotiable rules:\n- Written problem statement required with named decision owner\n- Every insertion requires an explicit swap-the executive names what gets cut\n- True emergencies (production outage, security breach, contractual obligation) bypass the gate but receive immediate tradeoff communication to all stakeholders\n\nPilot design: Pre/post comparison across three sprints (A/B impractical with a single team). Gate goes live sprint one; measure through sprint three.\n\nSuccess metrics:\n- Sprint goal completion rate: target ≥80% (establish baseline first)\n- Mid-sprint insertion frequency: target ≤1 per sprint\n- Velocity stability: coefficient of variation across 6 sprints\n- Executive satisfaction score: quarterly pulse, ensuring process isn't creating adversarial dynamics\n\nTrade-offs I'd own explicitly: This feels bureaucratic to executives with direct-access habits. Political friction is highest if the team is already underdelivering-enforcing discipline before demonstrating consistent shipping creates credibility risk. I'd phase this in after one sprint of visible wins, and position the gate as protecting executive priorities from each other, not protecting the team from executives.",
