    "q": "Tell me about a time you had to learn something new very quickly.",
    "a": "When I need to learn something fast, I use a structured gap-targeting framework: identify unknowns precisely, route to highest-signal sources, validate your model explicitly.\n\nIn my first week at a fintech company, I was handed integration planning for a payments API I'd never touched-engineering kickoff in five days.\n\nRather than reading broadly, I ran a 72-hour learning sprint with a clear success metric: can I produce an artifact that an expert will correct, not just confirm?\n\nDay 1: Full API documentation, logging every unknown term. Result: 23 specific questions, not general confusion. Day 2: 30-minute session with our lead engineer, but I came with tradeoff-framed questions-\"what breaks first under high retry volume?\"-not open-ended ones. Days 3-4: Built a one-page flow diagram and handed it back for redlining. Forcing active correction exposed faulty assumptions 3x faster than Q&A alone.\n\nThe deliberate trade-off: I deprioritized edge cases to validate the core flow first. Acceptable risk given the timeline; I flagged it explicitly to the engineer.\n\nDay 5 kickoff ran on schedule. The diagram surfaced an error-handling gap that would have cost a full sprint post-launch. That artifact later became standard onboarding material-an unplanned but measurable outcome.\n\nWhat I validated: fast learning isn't about volume. It's about knowing precisely what you don't know, then designing feedback loops that force correction rather than approval. I now apply this framework every time I enter a new product domain.",
